  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Wednesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Large upper level closed low continues \nto slowly meander east and into California this morning. The low \nwill concurrently weaken as it moves into Nevada today and \nThursday, opening into a deep upper level trof. Trof axis looks \nto be centered over Nevada by Thursday night, after midnight. By \nFriday morning, most of the energy associated with the system \nwill shift east into Utah as the trof splits into two pieces. The \nnorthern half lifting north into Montana and Alberta and a \nsouthern piece re- organizing into another closed low that will \ndrift from Arizona into the four corners region Saturday. \n\nSensible weather wise, periods of showers and thunderstorms will \ncontinue thru Friday morning, with activity dissipating from west \nto east Friday afternoon and thru Friday night as the afore \nmentioned upper level trof progresses east into Utah. Areal \ncoverage of showers and thunderstorms will again increase from the\nsouth, moving into central Nevada this afternoon and evening. The\nmain push of showers and thunderstorms will occur Thursday as the\nmain energy of the system passes overhead. Thunderstorms will be \npossible across the entire forecast area and there is a marginal \nrisk of severe thunderstorms across eastern Nevada, including \nWhite Pine and Elko counties. The potential for severe \nthunderstorms across eastern Nevada on Thursday looks top be \nsupported by adequate CAPE and mid level lapse rates, which will \nalso be co- located with the main area of upper level lift. Gusty \nand erratic outflow winds, frequent cloud to ground lightning, and\nsmall hail are the primary hazards. \n\nBy Friday morning cooler air aloft will filter in from the \nnorthwest, and snow levels which had been around 10,000 feet for \nthe majority of the event will lower to around 6,000 feet just as \nprecipitation begins to dissipate across northern Nevada from west\nto east. Timing may allow for snowfall which had been restricted \nto the mountain summits to reach some of the higher elevation \npasses on NV225 Friday morning. That said, any valley snowfall \nwithin northwest Elko County will be limited to less than an inch.\nStorm total rainfall accumulation for the four day period that \nbegan Tuesday and will run thru Friday continues to look robust \nwith most locations expected to receive between 0.25 inches and \n0.75 inches. One inch or more of liquid equivalent is expected for\nthe mountains thru Friday afternoon. \n\nBeyond Friday, high pressure looks to quickly build in behind the\ntrof, with the ridge firmly in control by Sunday. This will\nprovide fair weather and a modest warming trend into next week\nMonday. Ridge looks to remain in control thru Wednesday of next \nweek with fair weather continuing.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: There is high confidence for isolated\nto scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms today and tomorrow\nwith the majority of the activity occurring on Thursday.\n\nThere is high confidence in the return of upper ridging Saturday \nthrough Wednesday of next week. \n\nSignificant edits required within PoP and weather fields versus\nNBM output.",
